● This is very strange. If git ls-files shows the files, it means Git has them 
  recorded locally, and if git push says "Everything up-to-date", Git believes
  the server (GitHub) already has exactly what you have locally.

  If you still don't see them on GitHub.com, there are only two possibilities
  left:

  Possibility 1: You are looking at the wrong branch on GitHub

  GitHub often defaults to showing the master branch or some other branch.
  1. Go to your GitHub repository page.
  2. Look for the button that says "main" (usually on the top left of the file
     list).
  3. Click it and make sure main is selected. If you see a branch named master,
     switch to it and see if the files are there.

  Possibility 2: Your local Git is confused about the "Remote" (The most likely 
  cause)

  Your local Git might be pushing to a different repository than the one you are
  checking in your browser.

  Run this command to check where your code is actually going:
  git remote -v

  Check the output:
  Does the URL listed after origin match the exact URL of the repository you are
  checking in your browser?
  - Example: https://github.com/YourUsername/portfolio.git

  ---

  🚀 How to fix this once and for all

  If the URL is wrong, or if you just want to force GitHub to match your local
  files perfectly, do this:

  1. Remove the existing remote connection:
  git remote remove origin

  2. Add the CORRECT remote connection (Replace the URL with your actual GitHub
  repo URL):
  git remote add origin https://github.com/YOUR_USERNAME/YOUR_REPO_NAME.git

  3. Push your local code to the main branch:
  git push -u origin main --force

  After doing this, refresh your GitHub page. All the files from your git 
  ls-files list MUST appear there.

  Once they appear on GitHub, you can go back to Vercel and import the project
  again! Let me know what git remote -v says if you are unsure.

