● The URL is correct. If git ls-files shows the files and git push says
  "Everything up-to-date", but they aren't on GitHub, it means your local Git
  thinks the remote has them, but the remote (GitHub) actually doesn't. This can
  happen if the remote history was reset or modified.

  Let's try the most reliable way to "force" GitHub to accept your local
  version.

  🛠️ The "Clean Slate" Push

  Run these commands one by one. This will basically tell GitHub: "Ignore 
  everything you have, and take exactly what I have on my computer right now."

  1. First, let's make sure we are on the main branch:
  git checkout main

  2. Commit anything that might be lingering (just in case):
  git add .
  git commit -m "Final sync of all files"
  (If it says "nothing to commit", just move to the next step).

  3. Force push to the remote:
  git push origin main --force

  ---

  🧐 If that STILL doesn't work, try this (The "Reset Remote" Method):

  If the command above still says "Everything up-to-date" but the files are
  missing on GitHub, your local .git folder might be corrupted. Let's do a fresh
  initialize:

  ⚠️ Warning: This resets the git history but keeps your files.

  1. Delete the local git folder (don't worry, your code stays safe):
  rm -rf .git
     (If you are in PowerShell and rm -rf doesn't work, use: Remove-Item 
     -Recurse -Force .git)
  2. Re-initialize Git:
  git init
  3. Add all your files:
  git add .
  4. Commit the files:
  git commit -m "Fresh start: Full portfolio code"
  5. Connect to your GitHub repo again:
  git remote add origin https://github.com/Khan-coder-31/portfolio.git
  6. Force push to overwrite GitHub:
  git push -u origin main --force

  After this, refresh your GitHub page. The app folder and all other files MUST 
  be there.

  Once you confirm the files are on GitHub, go to Vercel and deploy. It will
  work! Let me know when you've tried this.
