● This is the final hurdle! Even though you've updated the IP whitelist and
  Environment Variables, Vercel is still failing because it is trying to
  Prerender (Static Generation) your home page during the build process.

  When Vercel builds your app, it runs npm run build. In your code, HomePage is
  an async function that calls await dbConnect() and fetches projects and
  experiences. If the connection takes too long or fails for even a second
  during the build, the whole deployment fails.

  🚀 The Fix: Switch to Dynamic Rendering

  The best way to solve this for portfolio sites is to tell Next.js not to try
  and build the page as a static HTML file during the build process, but instead
  to fetch the data when the user actually visits the site.

  Please apply this change to app/page.tsx:

  1. Open app/page.tsx.
  2. Add this line at the very top (or just after the imports):
  export const dynamic = 'force-dynamic';

  Why this fixes it:

  By adding export const dynamic = 'force-dynamic', you are telling Vercel:
  "Don't try to connect to MongoDB during the build process. Wait until a user 
  actually visits the page, then connect to the database." This bypasses the
  "Prerendering" phase where your error is happening.

  ---

  🛠️ Final Action Plan:

  1. Edit the file:
  // app/page.tsx
  import { ExternalLink, Github } from 'lucide-react';
  // ... other imports

  export const dynamic = 'force-dynamic'; // <--- ADD THIS LINE

  export default async function HomePage() {
    await dbConnect();
    // ... rest of the code
  }
  2. Push the change to GitHub:
  git add app/page.tsx
  git commit -m "Fix: force dynamic rendering to bypass build-time DB 
  connection"
  git push origin main
  3. Check Vercel:
     Vercel will automatically detect the push and start a new deployment. This
     time, it will skip the prerendering of the home page and should finish
     successfully!

  Try this now, and your portfolio should finally be live! 🌟

