● Don't worry, this is not a bug in the code. ChunkLoadError is a very common
  Next.js development error.

  It basically means that while you were running the server, the "chunks" (small
  pieces of code) in the .next folder got out of sync or the browser is trying
  to load an old version of the page that was cached.

  🛠️ How to fix this (The "Clean Start" Method):

  Follow these 3 steps exactly:

  1. Stop the server:
  Go to your terminal and press Ctrl + C to stop the npm run dev process.

  2. Delete the .next folder (Clear Cache):
  This folder contains the compiled build. Sometimes it gets corrupted during
  development. Deleting it forces Next.js to rebuild everything from scratch.
  Run this command in your terminal:
  rm -rf .next
  (If rm -rf doesn't work in your terminal, just manually right-click the .next 
  folder in VS Code and Delete it).

  3. Start the server again:
  npm run dev

  ---

  🔍 Why this happens:

  When we add a new file like template.tsx or change layout.tsx, Next.js tries
  to update the "chunks" of code. If the browser is still holding onto the old
  "chunk" but the server has a new one, it creates a ChunkLoadError.

  Deleting the .next folder is like a "Factory Reset" for your local build.

  Try these steps and then refresh your browser. The error should be gone, and 
  you should see the smooth animations! Let me know if it works. 😊

