● Ah, I understand now! You are talking about the Loading State
  (app/loading.tsx).

  You are seeing the "Loading Portfolio" icon for a split second before the page
  appears. This is actually a built-in feature of Next.js. Because we are using
  async components to fetch data from MongoDB, Next.js shows the loading.tsx
  file while the database is responding.

  🧐 Is this a problem?

  No, it's actually a good thing! It tells the user that the site is working and
  just fetching data, rather than showing a blank white screen.

  However, if you feel it's distracting or you want a more professional feel, we
  have two options:

  Option 1: Keep it (Professional Standard)

  Almost every modern app (Facebook, YouTube, etc.) uses "skeleton loaders" or
  loading icons. It's the standard way to handle data fetching.

  Option 2: Make the Loading State more "Invisible"

  We can make the loading.tsx page look more like a "ghost" of your actual site
  (called Skeleton Screens) or just make it a very simple, fast fade.

  ---

  🏁 Verdict on Transitions:

  Since the pages are loading, the animations are working, and the loading icon
